::: {.doc-subject}

Perform breadth-first search (BFS) traversal from a start node.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L68632-L68793){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.bfs(
    source,
    attr_name=None,
    return_list=False,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Breadth-first search](../assets/graphs/bfs.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="A six-node graph searched breadth-first from A, with nodes in columns by distance from A (0 to 3). Numbered badges give the visit order, which finishes each column before starting the next, and the search tree's edges are in the accent color. The result card lists the order A, B, C, D, E, F." width="100%"}

BFS explores the graph layer by layer: it visits the start node, then all of its
neighbors, then all of their unvisited neighbors, and so on. Nodes are therefore reached
in order of their hop distance from `source=`. Within a layer, neighbors are visited in the
order their edges were added. On a directed graph only outgoing edges are followed, so
the traversal covers exactly the nodes reachable from `source=`; nodes that cannot be
reached are never visited.

Common reasons to run a BFS:

- listing everything reachable from a node, closest first
- ordering nodes by hop distance for layered layouts or animations
- checking which part of a graph a given node can reach

By default the visit order is stored on the graph as a node attribute and the graph is
returned for chaining. Set `return_list=True` to get the visit order directly instead.
Either way, the traversal is also recorded in the graph's step log (`steps`), so it can
be replayed node by node with `play()`.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[source]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The node to start the traversal from, given as an integer node ID or a string label.
    It is always the first node visited (position `0`).

<code>[attr_name]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Name of the node attribute that receives each node's position in the traversal. If
    `None` (default), the attribute is called `bfs_order`. Positions are 0-indexed, so the
    start node gets `0`, the first neighbor visited gets `1`, and so on. Nodes the
    traversal does not reach get no value. An existing attribute with the same name is
    overwritten. Ignored when `return_list=True`.

<code>[return_list]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[False]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   If `False` (default), store the positions as a node attribute (see `attr_name=`) and
    return the graph for method chaining. If `True`, leave node attributes untouched and
    return the list of visited node IDs in BFS order.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Union[list[int], Graph]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If `return_list=` is `True`: list of node IDs visited in BFS order, starting with
    `source=`. If `return_list=` is `False` (default): the graph itself, for method
    chaining.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If `source=` does not exist in the graph.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~dfs`) 

:   Visit nodes depth-first, following each branch to its end.

[](`~bfs_layers`) 

:   Group the nodes by hop distance from the sources.

[](`~bfs_edges`), [](`~bfs_tree`) 

:   The edges of the search tree, as a list or a new graph.

[](`~single_source_shortest_path_length`) 

:   Distances that take edge weights into account.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

Starting from A, BFS visits A's neighbors B and C before going any further, then the nodes
two steps away, D and E, and finally F. Each node's place in that order is stored in the
`bfs_order` column:

::: {#bdb4971d .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [("A", "B"), ("A", "C"), ("B", "D"), ("C", "E"), ("D", "F")]
)

graph.bfs("A").get_node_df().select("label", "bfs_order")
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```{=html}
<div><style>
.dataframe > thead > tr,
.dataframe > tbody > tr {
  text-align: right;
  white-space: pre-wrap;
}
</style>
<small>shape: (6, 2)</small><table border="1" class="dataframe"><thead><tr><th>label</th><th>bfs_order</th></tr><tr><td>str</td><td>i64</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;A&quot;</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;B&quot;</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;C&quot;</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;D&quot;</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;E&quot;</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;F&quot;</td><td>5</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
```
:::
:::


`return_list=True` returns the node IDs in visit order instead:

::: {#1e49c121 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
graph.bfs("A", return_list=True)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]
```
:::
:::


### Watching the search spread

Every traversal is recorded in the step log, so `play()` can replay it one visit at a time.
Here BFS starts from Central station on the metro map, which stores each station's map
position in `pos`. Step through to see the frontier move outward, finishing every station
one stop away before it reaches any that are two stops away:

::: {#a9d0f7b9 .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
metro = ec.metro()

metro.note("Visit every station one stop away first.").bfs("Central")
metro.play(layout="pos")
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}
